{
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE CLOUD DEPLOYMENT — TWO ENVIRONMENTS, ONE LADDER.
     51 §4.6 writes this file in full for a single environment. This is that file with `alpha`
     and `production` split out of it, and the split is not cosmetic: 33 §7 and 31 §8 both say
     plainly *"there is no alpha environment: the ops record is the canary"*, and 53 §10 records
     the ceiling with its own exit — `ponytail: no alpha environment ... add env.alpha in
     wrangler.jsonc when a second person deploys`. This is that day, taken early. The canary
     argument is untouched: the ops record is still the first record to migrate on every deploy
     (53 §5.3). Alpha is not a replacement for it — it is where a DEPLOY is rehearsed, and the
     ops record is where a MIGRATION is. Amend 33 §7, 31 §8 and 53 §13 #14 in the same revision.

     THE FRONTEND IS NOT DEPLOYED SEPARATELY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS FILE. `assets`
     below carries `apps/web/build` INTO the Worker version (33 §7, 50 §13 D2). One `wrangler
     deploy` ships the shell and the code as one version, which is what lets `deploy-parity`
     compare the live `/` against the local `build/` and mean something (52 §3): if they were two
     deploys they could differ for a minute and the gate would be measuring a race. So the answer
     to *"how is the static frontend deployed"* is: it is not, it is a property of the Worker
     version. Only `apps/site` is a separate static deployment, and it has its own file.

     ORDER OF THE KEYS BELOW: everything inheritable first, then the two environments. The
     division is not stylistic — it is the one fact this file has to get right. See the two
     comment blocks at `migrations` and at `env`.
     =========================================================================================== */

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE APP IS BACK ON ITS OWN NAME: THIS FILE IS PUSH C. It reverses the one-domain move's last
     step and nothing else. The app lives on `app.brain.dog` (alpha: `app.alpha.brain.dog`), at its
     root since 29 Oct (it was under `/app` until then), and `brain.dog` and `alpha.brain.dog` are
     legacy hosts again.

     PUSH C, PRODUCTION HERE: `PUBLIC_HOST` is `app.brain.dog` and `LEGACY_HOSTS` is `brain.dog`, and
     no route changed. `app.brain.dog` has stayed this Worker's Custom Domain throughout, so C is live
     the moment this Worker deploys: every cookie-authenticated write must now carry
     `Origin: https://app.brain.dog`, so everybody signs in once more (the `__Host-` cookie is bound to
     the name that set it). A page load on `brain.dog/app/...` reaches this Worker through the site's
     binding and is one 308 to `https://app.brain.dog/...`, the `/app` taken off and the rest and the
     query kept, and `/api/*`, `/mcp`
     and `/in/*` on `brain.dog` are served and never redirected, so tokens and `/in/` addresses handed
     out there keep working. A device start on `brain.dog` is refused and names `app.brain.dog`.
     Passkeys keep `RP_ID` `brain.dog`, which is a registrable suffix of `app.brain.dog`, so none is
     stranded. The repository variable `DEPLOY_HOST` has to be `app.brain.dog` before this push deploys,
     so the Parity step asks the name the app lives on. ROLLBACK is a version rollback of this Worker,
     or the four values swapped back.

     PUSH C, ALPHA HERE: the same on alpha's names, `PUBLIC_HOST` `app.alpha.brain.dog` and
     `LEGACY_HOSTS` `alpha.brain.dog`.

     THE SHARED NAME REACHES THIS WORKER THROUGH THE SITE'S SERVICE BINDING, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE
     CHANGES FOR IT. The site keeps `brain.dog` (and `alpha.brain.dog`) as its Custom Domain, runs its
     script first on this Worker's doors (its `assets.run_worker_first`) and hands each of those
     requests to this Worker through `services: APP` (`apps/site/wrangler.jsonc`). Measured through
     `wrangler dev -c site -c app`: every door answered with this Worker's own answer, `Host`, `Origin`, `Cookie` and `cf-connecting-ip` arrived as
     sent, and `POST /auth/device` on `brain.dog` passed the `PUBLIC_HOST` check, so this Worker
     cannot tell it is behind the binding. Two reasons it replaced the zone routes that were drafted
     here. NO ROUTE IS WRITTEN, so the deploy token needs no Zone > Workers Routes > Edit on
     `brain.dog`, which only the owner can grant: the site deploy it adds is a script and a binding
     on a Worker `deploy.yml` already uploads with the same token. And ROLLBACK IS A VERSION
     ROLLBACK: everything the move adds lives in the site's version, where a zone route lived outside
     any version and came off only by hand. `scripts/provision-check.mjs`'s `siteBinding` is what
     counts a site name as this Worker's, and only when the site's same environment binds
     `braindog-<env>` and runs first on every built door.

     EACH ENVIRONMENT KEEPS ITS OLD NAME AS A LEGACY HOST rather than dropping it, because dropping a
     Custom Domain is a route change and a flip must make none. The old names go later, together,
     after the 90 days a token lives and once every `/in/` address handed out on them is re-minted.

     THE FORMAT, FOR A LATER MOVE. Nothing is staged now. A push still to come is written here as
     `STAGE <name> { ... }`, JSON applied on top of the push before it: a `routes` list replaces the
     environment's list, and `vars` sets the keys it names and keeps the rest. `pushes` in
     `scripts/provision-check.mjs` reads it, `test/wrangler-routes.test.ts` holds it to the same
     checks as the file, and `scripts/smoke.test.mjs` pins what the smoke step asks after it. To make
     the push, copy its fragment into the environment block below, delete it here, and rename the
     file in this block's first sentence.
     =========================================================================================== */

  "name": "braindog",
  "main": "src/index.ts",

  /* INHERITABLE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT OVERRIDDEN PER ENVIRONMENT. A alpha environment on a
     different compatibility date is not a rehearsal of production, it is a rehearsal of a
     different runtime — and the one thing this date decides is whether `deleteAll()` also deletes
     the alarm (51 A7, floor 2026-02-24), which is precisely the behaviour a customer deletion
     drill exercises (53 §5.9). One date, both environments, and `compat-date` reads this one key
     (52 §3). */
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",

  /* No `nodejs_compat`: `node:sqlite` is a stub under it (22 §9) and nothing here reaches for
     `node:*`. An empty array rather than an absent key, so a flag added later is a diff. */
  "compatibility_flags": [],

  /* BOTH OFF, IN BOTH ENVIRONMENTS, AND THIS IS A SECURITY SETTING RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
     `workers.dev` and preview URLs are additional ORIGINS serving the same records. 51 §5.1 step 3
     makes every cookie-authenticated mutation require `Origin === "https://" + PUBLIC_HOST`, so a
     second origin is either a surface where nothing can be written (confusing) or, if anyone ever
     "fixes" it by relaxing the check, a CSRF hole with a stable public hostname (12 §8). It is
     also the reason there are no per-pull-request preview deployments. */
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,

  /* 53 §2.3's five `op` lines are read here. Inheritable, so both environments get it, which is
     what makes a alpha incident debuggable with the same `wrangler tail` the runbook names. */
  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  /* FIVE MINUTES OF CPU, NOT THE DEFAULT THIRTY SECONDS. On 18 Oct a record's alarm ran past thirty seconds and the
     object was reset, failing every request waiting on it (`/api/whoami` among them). Only CPU spent is billed, so the
     ceiling costs nothing until a pass needs it, and `Record$`'s `alarm-step` line names the pass that did. Inheritable. */
  "limits": { "cpu_ms": 300000 },

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE STATIC SHELL. Inheritable, so both environments serve the same `build/` — there is one
     artefact and it is host-agnostic by construction (50 §2.1, §13 D1/D2).

     `directory` IS RELATIVE TO THIS FILE, which is why it climbs. 51 §4.6 writes
     `apps/worker/src/index.ts` and `apps/web/build` because it imagines the config at the
     repository root; the file actually lives in `apps/worker/`, so both paths shorten by one
     segment and the asset directory gains a `..`. Amend 51 §4.6's two paths, or move the file —
     not both.

     `run_worker_first` IS 33 §4'S LIST AND NOT A SECOND COPY OF IT. Everything outside it is the
     app's (19 §5: *if a future change requires enumerating app paths in two files, that change is
     wrong*) — this enumerates the HOST's paths, which is the complement and is allowed to exist
     once. Several of these routes are not built yet, and listing them anyway is the truthful
     choice: `src/route.ts`'s `servesApi` sends an unbuilt one to `ROUTE_UNKNOWN` (404 JSON),
     whereas leaving it off this list serves `index.html` with a 200 to an agent's POST — a lie
     that looks like a working endpoint. `/.well-known/*` is on the list because 33 §7's skeleton
     has it and `braindog verify` reads `/.well-known/braindog-key` offline (03 §4); the SPA shell
     answering there would break verification of every backup.

     THE APP IS AT THE ROOT, AND THE WORKER RUNS FIRST ON EVERY PATH (`/*`). `/<slug>/...` names the
     record in the path, which the Worker has to check against the Directory before any id is used, so
     no file lookup may answer an app navigation before the Worker has seen it. The doors above are under
     `/*` and are not listed again: wrangler refuses an entry another one makes redundant ("'/api/*': rule '/*'
     makes it redundant"), so the host's paths are said by `src/route.ts` and `DOORS` in
     `scripts/provision-check.mjs`, and the site's list still names each door it hands over. It was `/app`
     and `/app/*` until 29 Oct, when the app left `/app` because he did not want the second `app`; the
     Worker answers every address under `/app` with a 308 to the same address without it. Two
     negatives take precedence over `/*` ("Negative patterns have precedence over non-negative
     patterns", developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/binding): `_app` is the build's hashed
     chunks, and `catalogue` is the bundled catalogue, which `test/bundled-catalogue.test.ts` holds to
     being answered by the file and never by the Worker. The app is built at the root of `directory`,
     so the path and the folder agree.

     `not_found_handling` IS `none`, AND IT WAS `single-page-application`. That fallback answered
     every miss with `index.html` and a 200, which would draw the app shell for a POST under
     `/<slug>/api/` the router has no method for. With `none` a miss reaches the Worker's code, which
     developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/routing/worker-script states for a Worker with a
     script: when no asset is found, the Worker is invoked. The Worker draws the shell for an app
     navigation itself. */
  "assets": {
    "directory": "../web/build",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "none",
    "run_worker_first": [
      "/*",
      "!/_app/*",
      "!/catalogue/*"
    ]
  },

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     DURABLE OBJECT MIGRATIONS — THE PART THAT GOES WRONG, SO IT IS THE PART WITH THE LONGEST
     COMMENT. Six facts, in the order they bite:

     1. `migrations` IS AN INHERITABLE KEY, and the migration tag is tracked PER WORKER SCRIPT.
        A named environment is a separate script — `braindog-alpha` and `braindog-production` —
        so each one keeps its own list of applied tags while reading this one ladder. That is
        exactly what is wanted: one ladder, two scripts, each climbing it independently.

     2. THEREFORE THE LADDER LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Cloudflare's rule is that a migration
        block inside an environment REPLACES the top-level one; it does not merge. A `migrations`
        key under `env.alpha` is how the two environments silently stop being the same product.
        There is no legitimate reason to write one. If you ever find yourself wanting one, what you
        actually want is a new tag here.

     3. THE ARRAY IS APPEND-ONLY AND EVERY PAST TAG STAYS IN IT FOREVER. Tags are unique names,
        not versions; wrangler applies the ones this script has not applied. Editing an applied tag
        in place is the quietest failure available — the deploy succeeds, the tag is already
        recorded, and nothing runs.

     4. A NEW ENVIRONMENT REPLAYS THE WHOLE LADDER FROM EMPTY. `braindog-alpha` on its first
        deploy applies v1, then v2, then v3, in order, against no data. Every tag must therefore be
        valid against an empty script — which is a live constraint on `renamed_classes`, whose
        `from` class will not exist there. Create alpha BEFORE the first rename lands and the
        problem never arises, because both scripts then walk the same rungs in the same order.
        (Whether a rename of an absent class is a no-op or an error is still to be verified.)

     5. NEVER `deleted_classes`. 12 §8 and 53 §3 both state it as a rule rather than a preference:
        it deletes every object of that class and the data in them, and there is no undo. A class
        that is finished is left bound and unused.

     6. A NEW TAG BLOCKS `wrangler rollback` — Cloudflare refuses a rollback across a Durable
        Object class lifecycle change. This is a SECOND rule on top of 53 §5.2's own (*after a
        kernel migration a rollback is forbidden; every record that migrated answers the old code
        with SCHEMA_NEWER*), and the two are independent: the platform's rule is about the class
        list in this array, the corpus's is about `packages/kernel/migrations/`. Either one alone
        makes the deploy a fix-forward-only deploy.

     THE CLASS NAMES ARE THE CODE'S, NOT 51 §4.6'S. `src/index.ts` exports `Record$` and
     `Directory$`; the `$` is there because `Record` is a TypeScript utility type and the class
     would shadow it. 51 §4.6 writes `Record`, `Slash`, `Directory`. A config naming a class the
     script does not export does not deploy, so this file follows the code and 51 §4.6 is the thing
     to amend. THIS IS FREE TO DECIDE EXACTLY ONCE — before the first production deploy. Afterwards
     the two names cost a `renamed_classes` tag.

     `Slash$` IS NOT HERE BECAUSE THE CLASS IS NOT WRITTEN. 33 §7 lists three classes; the code has
     two. Its rung is drafted below so the shape is settled before it is needed. */
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Record$", "Directory$"] }

    /* THE NEXT RUNG, WHEN `src/do/slash.ts` LANDS (51 §2, §3). Append; do not edit v1.
       { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Slash$"] } */

    /* AND IF 51 §4.6'S NAMES WIN AFTER A PRODUCTION DEPLOY, this is the shape — a rename, never
       an edit of v1, and never a delete:
       { "tag": "v3", "renamed_classes": [
           { "from": "Record$", "to": "Record" }, { "from": "Directory$", "to": "Directory" } ] } */
  ],

  /* THE NIGHTLY WALK, 53 §5.1, AND ITS TRIGGER ARRIVED IN ONE CHANGE, which is what the note that
     stood here asked for: a cron pointed at a Worker with no `scheduled()` is a nightly invocation
     that does nothing and says nothing. `src/index.ts` exports one now, over `src/nightly.ts` and
     `src/snapshots.ts`, and it throws when `BLOBS` is unbound rather than returning quietly.

     IT IS AT THE TOP LEVEL RATHER THAN PER-ENVIRONMENT. Inheritable means both environments run the
     walk, and that is the point: 53 §9 wants the drills rehearsed, and a backup path that has only
     ever run in production is a backup path nobody has watched fail. Alpha writes to its own R2
     bucket, so the two nightly runs cannot touch each other.

     BEFORE THE NEXT DEPLOY, THE TWO BUCKETS BELOW HAVE TO EXIST. Binding a bucket that does not
     exist fails the deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs` and
     `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs-alpha`, once each, on the account. */
  "triggers": { "crons": ["17 3 * * *"] },

  /* INBOUND MAIL NEEDS NO LINE IN THIS FILE, AND THIS NOTE IS WHERE THE STEP THAT DOES LIVES. `src/index.ts`
     exports `email()`, which hands each message to the same door as `POST /in/email/<token>` (`src/email.ts`), and an
     Email Routing rule is what sends a domain's mail to it. That rule is his DNS step, per environment, and nothing
     reaches `email()` before it: turn on Email Routing for a receiving subdomain (the MX records Cloudflare adds), then
     one catch-all rule, action "Send to a Worker", naming this Worker's script for that environment
     (`braindog` or `braindog-alpha`). The address a record hands its senders is then `<token>@<that subdomain>`, the
     token the Ingest screen mints today. Until the rule exists the HTTP door is the only one, as it has been. */

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE TWO ENVIRONMENTS.

     THE ONE RULE: EVERY NON-INHERITABLE KEY IS REPEATED IN FULL IN BOTH BLOCKS. `durable_objects`,
     `queues`, `r2_buckets`, `vars`, `secrets_store_secrets`, `analytics_engine_datasets`,
     `send_email`, `services` and `kv_namespaces` are non-inheritable, and a non-inheritable key
     omitted from an environment is not inherited — it is EMPTY. The deploy succeeds. The Worker
     comes up with no Durable Object binding, or no queue, and the first request is the error
     report. Duplication here is not redundancy, it is the schema.

     `routes` IS INHERITABLE, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME TRAP AND THE MORE DANGEROUS ONE.
     A `routes` key at the top level of this file would be inherited by `env.alpha`, and
     `wrangler deploy --env alpha` would then claim `app.brain.dog`. Two Workers cannot hold one
     Custom Domain, so the likeliest outcome is a failed deploy, but the outcome that is not ruled
     out is alpha answering production's hostname. There is deliberately no top-level `routes` key
     above. Routes are declared here and only here.

     AND THERE IS NO NAMELESS DEPLOY. Because every binding lives inside an environment, a bare
     `wrangler deploy` would upload a third Worker called `braindog` with no bindings at all.
     `deploy.yml` always passes `--env`; do the same by hand. (`wrangler` is expected to refuse
     this outright, since `migrations` names classes the environment does not bind — *verify*.)
     =========================================================================================== */
  "env": {
    /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       ALPHA — script `braindog-alpha`. Deployed on every green merge to master, by nobody.
       Its Durable Objects, its queue, its bucket and its KEK are its own; there is no path by
       which a alpha request reaches a production record, because a DO namespace is scoped to
       the script and these are two scripts.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    "alpha": {
      /* PUSH B1: ALPHA LIVES ON `alpha.brain.dog`, AND `app.alpha.brain.dog` STAYS ITS ONLY CUSTOM
         DOMAIN, WITH NO ZONE ROUTE. It is the shape B2 gives production, on alpha's names, one push
         early. `alpha.brain.dog` reaches this Worker through the alpha site's `APP` binding, which is
         not in this file (the block at the top says why), and `test/wrangler-routes.test.ts` holds the
         two environments to the same paths in every push. The old name stays for the doors handed out
         on it, as production's will after B2.

         THIS BLOCK CAN GO LIVE WITHOUT `deploy.yml`. Alpha deploys from `deploy.yml` only when
         `DEPLOY_ALPHA` is set, but the Workers Builds connection `braindog-alpha` deploys it about
         ninety seconds after every push to master, before `ci` has passed and with no smoke after it.
         So B1 is on alpha whether or
         not the alpha site was ever deployed with its binding, and push 2 is made only after push 1's
         checks passed on the live `alpha.brain.dog`. It asks for no new permission either way.

         TWO LABELS DEEP HAS A CERTIFICATE. Universal SSL covers the apex and first-level names only;
         `brain.dog` carries an active advanced certificate for `*.alpha.brain.dog`, checked in the
         dashboard. Once the old name is dropped after B1, that certificate covers no live name, and
         whether to delete it is the owner's call. */
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "app.alpha.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }],

      /* ALPHA'S SHELL IS ITS OWN BUILD, `BRAINDOG_ENV=alpha` into `build-alpha`, so its accent differs
         from production's. An `assets` key inside an environment REPLACES the top-level one rather
         than merging, so every field is repeated: keep it identical to the top level but for the
         directory. */
      "assets": {
        "directory": "../web/build-alpha",
        "binding": "ASSETS",
        "not_found_handling": "none",
        "run_worker_first": [
          "/*",
          "!/_app/*",
          "!/catalogue/*"
        ]
      },

      /* `PUBLIC_HOST` IS THE CSRF CHECK. 51 §5.1 step 3 compares `Origin` against
         `https://<PUBLIC_HOST>` on every cookie-authenticated mutation, so a `PUBLIC_HOST` copied
         from production means every write in alpha is a 403 `FORBIDDEN` and every read works —
         a failure that looks like a permissions bug for about an hour.

         `AGE_RECIPIENT` IS A SEPARATE RECIPIENT, not a copy. 12 §8 keeps the age identity outside
         Cloudflare in the operator's password manager; two recipients means a alpha snapshot can
         never be opened with the production identity, and the restore drill (53 §5.5) exercises a
         real key rather than the real key.

         `AI_GATEWAY` is its own gateway so alpha's neuron spend and its rate limits are visible
         apart from production's (12 §8: *per record at AI Gateway*).

         `BRAINDOG_BUILD` IS DECLARED EMPTY AND OVERWRITTEN AT DEPLOY TIME by `--var` in
         `deploy.yml`. It is here so the key exists, so `wrangler types` emits it, and so the
         failure when the workflow forgets it is `""` — which `deploy-parity` catches (52 §13 #17)
         — rather than an undefined binding. It is a var 51 §4.1's `Env` does not list; adding it
         is an amendment, and `/api/version` cannot answer without it. */
      "vars": {
        "PUBLIC_HOST": "app.alpha.brain.dog",
        /* THE OLD NAME, A CUSTOM DOMAIN IN `routes` ABOVE, or nothing reaches the Worker to redirect. A page
           load under `/app` there is a 308 to `https://app.alpha.brain.dog<path>` with the `/app` taken
           off, and `/api/*`, `/mcp` and `/in/*` are
           served, never redirected, for the reasons production's comment gives. */
        "LEGACY_HOSTS": "alpha.brain.dog",
        /* THE PASSKEY'S RELYING PARTY, the parent of the host from the first deploy, so a passkey made on
           `app.alpha.brain.dog` is offered on `alpha.brain.dog` after B1: both ceremonies named
           `alpha.brain.dog` in the clone of push 2. Production's comment says why it never moves. */
        "RP_ID": "alpha.brain.dog",
        "AI_GATEWAY": "braindog-alpha",
        "AGE_RECIPIENT": "age1f6rykmshc4zf5y3lf0ecuyzyqr8a4ek29wlmp688gydyxckwjq3s75n58r",
        "SLASH_ENDPOINT": "",
        /* THE INCLUDED MODEL IS ON, AT THE OWNER'S WORD. `apps/worker/src/slash.ts` answers
           with Workers AI only when this is `yes`; the binding alone never switched it on. One call may spend about 2,500
           neurons (about $0.03) before it is stopped, and one record's asks at most the ceiling below in a month. */
        "SLASH_INCLUDED": "yes",
        /* THE OPERATOR'S CEILING ON ONE RECORD'S ASKS OF Slash, IN NEURONS A UTC MONTH: 300,000 is about $3.30 at $0.011 a
           thousand (down from 900,000 and about $9.90). The 80 asks a month a record is
           shown fit well inside it. It was the record owner's `ask_cap` alone, with no upper limit, and an owner saved a
           billion; `ask_cap` can now only lower this. It is per record
           and not per account. `INCLUDED_MONTH_NEURONS` in `apps/worker/src/slash.ts` is what an unset or mistyped
           value falls back to; `0` turns every ask away. THE CEILING HOLDS A PERSON'S ASKS AND THE RECORD'S OWN
           BACKGROUND ASKS APART, each to this number of asks, AND THE TWO TOGETHER TO THIS NUMBER OF NEURONS, so neither
           spends the other's and one record never costs more than it. */
        "SLASH_INCLUDED_MONTH_NEURONS": "300000",
        /* THE ROUTE: SLASH_LARGE is the operator's yes to the larger model's cost, and empty is no. The three after it name
           the operator's second provider, asked when the included model fails, with its secret SLASH_FALLBACK_KEY; any
           one empty is no second provider, and they stay empty until the published legal text names it. */
        "SLASH_LARGE": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_URL": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_MODEL": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_PROVIDER": "",
        "BRAINDOG_BUILD": "",
        /* EMPTY IS NO SIGN-IN LINKS. The sender, on a domain Email Service sends for, switches them on
           with the `EMAIL` binding below (51 §5.8). */
        "AUTH_MAIL_FROM": "hello@brain.dog",
        /* EMPTY FALLS BACK TO AUTH_MAIL_FROM: mail.ts reads it for everything but a sign-in link. */
        "MAIL_FROM": "",
        /* EMPTY FALLS BACK TO THE GENERIC FOOTER: mail.ts reads it for the address a reader is told to write to. */
        "SUPPORT_ADDRESS": "support@brain.dog",
        /* SUPPORT_FORWARD (where that address is forwarded) IS NOT HERE: it is his own inbox, set with
           `wrangler secret put SUPPORT_FORWARD`, and the address must be verified under Email Routing > Destination addresses. */
        /* AND THE ONE WORD THAT LETS A KEYLESS RECORD BE MADE ON PURPOSE. Empty is the default and
           the default is a refusal: with no `KEK` bound, `/auth/provision` answers
           `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` rather than making a record that can never seal a grant, because a
           record is given its keys once and the moment is the create. `yes` and nothing else turns
           that off, for a host that seals nothing. Set here or with
           `--var PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS:yes` on the deploy. */
        "PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS": "",
        /* TEAMS_HELD: `1` holds new invitations and joins on every plan (`TEAMS_HELD_SAID`), the switch to pull if sharing
           has to stop at once. Off since 1 Nov: member row rules, private rows, shares and the Admin's limits shipped, and
           a second person joins as the plan allows (`teamsStop` in `src/index.ts`), on any plan while billing is off. */
        "TEAMS_HELD": "",
        /* WEB PUSH'S PUBLIC KEY, AND EMPTY IS NO PUSH. It is a var and not a secret because the app hands it to every
           browser that subscribes. The private half is the secret `VAPID_PRIVATE` (the note at the end of this file),
           and `notify.push` is leased only when both are set (`delivers` in `src/deliver.ts`), so one without the
           other leaves pushes `pending`, as they have always been. Generated at the owner's word,
           one pair per environment; the private half lives only in the Worker secret and in his secrets
           folder, never in this repository. */
        "VAPID_PUBLIC": "BOG2T44cb4AumYSqEj5lD1-MyUYp3nD4iABd3Z5oEe_CoivSr_c6dyRmu02G_bAwlhnp6ZPJW5_wmZlNkJJclS0",
        /* BILLING IS OFF, AND THIS IS ITS SWITCH (`src/billing.ts`). Empty is off: the
           billing list says `open: false`, every other billing door answers `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` naming this setting,
           and the sign-up page asks nothing. `test` or `live` is on, and must agree with the prefix of the
           `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` secret (`sk_test_` or `sk_live_`) or every billing door refuses, naming both. */
        "BILLING": "test",
        /* THE STRIPE PRICES OF THE TWO PLANS, `price_...`, one per environment. Empty, and that plan is not sold: Cloud
           at $12 a month and Cloud Plus at $29, the words in `HOSTED_PLANS` (`src/billing.ts`) and the choice in
           `apps/site/src/lib/plans.ts`. What a card is charged is whatever the Stripe price says. */
        "STRIPE_PRICE_HOSTED": "price_1UWyb4PzwPNEI3xxnKVyIRxZ",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PLUS": "price_1UWyb5PzwPNEI3xx2wLtpXmd",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PACK_100": "price_1UWyb6PzwPNEI3xxk4kmImtq",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PACK_500": "price_1UWyb7PzwPNEI3xxbhlFyxhN",
        /* EXTRA USAGE'S METERED PRICE AND ITS METER, and STRIPE TAX (`on` adds tax on Stripe's page). Empty is not sold / off.
           `node tools/ship/stripe.mjs` fills the price and the meter; STRIPE_TAX is the owner's switch once Stripe Tax is set up. */
        "STRIPE_PRICE_EXTRA": "price_1UWyb9PzwPNEI3xx2babBohE",
        "STRIPE_METER_EXTRA": "braindog_extra_cents",
        "STRIPE_TAX": "",
        /* WHO MAY MAKE AN ACCOUNT, AND THIS IS ITS ONE SWITCH (`src/launch.ts`). `open` is anybody, `invite` is a new
           address with a code from `/account/ops/invite`, and anything else, empty included, is closed. Open here as in
           production, so the walk kit signs up with no code, as a stranger does. */
        "SIGNUP": "open",
        /* THE SIGN-UP PAGE'S TURNSTILE WIDGET. Empty, and sign-up runs on the Directory's lockouts alone, tightened to
           three an hour from one address until the secret half, `TURNSTILE_SECRET` (named at the end of this file),
           is set; `/healthz` says so in its `launch` sentence. */
        "TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY": "",
        /* FIRST_READ_DAILY: the day's ceiling of sign-up readings by the included model for all strangers, and the operator's yes to them. Unset is off, and the page reads by rules. 300, his yes to C7 (1 Nov, "i need it highly intelligent"). Still off in effect until `TURNSTILE_SECRET` is set: the door reads nothing behind no bot check. */
        "FIRST_READ_DAILY": "300",
        /* SHARE_LINKS: `on` makes read-only links to a page (`sharesOf` in `src/share.ts`), with `KEK` bound. Unset, and none are made, and every open link stops answering. On since 1 Nov (his ask of 20 Oct, sharing finished). */
        "SHARE_LINKS": "on",
        /* COUNT_VISITS: `yes` counts the site's visits by day, page and where they came from, and nothing about who (`src/seen.ts`). Unset, and `/api/seen` answers 204 and counts nothing. Unset in production, the owner's choice. */
        /* THE OPERATOR'S CANARY RECORD, a `rec_...` id. Empty, and the nightly walk writes its summary nowhere and
           `deploy.yml`'s sandbox smoke says it was skipped. EMPTY ON PURPOSE UNTIL HIS CANARY SITTING:
           `tools/canary/enrol.mjs`, run by him, makes
           the record `ops` through the public sign-up and prints the one line that fills this. The loop never fills
           it by hand, since a record it named would be a canary nobody enrolled. */
        "OPS_RECORD": ""
      },

      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "RECORD", "class_name": "Record$" },
          /* One instance for the whole installation, always `idFromName('directory')`. It is the
             tenant map, and two of them would be two maps that disagree — ADR-061. */
          { "name": "DIRECTORY", "class_name": "Directory$" }
        ]
      },

      /* A QUEUE NAME IS ACCOUNT-GLOBAL, so it carries the environment. Sharing one queue between
         two scripts would have alpha's consumer leasing production's outbox rows — the message
         is `{ record }` and nothing else (01 §2, 51 §13 D16), so the consumer would resolve that
         id against its own namespace, find an empty record, and ack nothing. Quiet, and wrong. */
      "queues": {
        "producers": [{ "queue": "braindog-ingress-alpha", "binding": "INGRESS" }],
        "consumers": [
          {
            "queue": "braindog-ingress-alpha",
            "max_batch_size": 25,
            "max_batch_timeout": 5,
            /* Cloudflare's own retry, over the record's ladder — this one covers a consumer that
               crashed. A delivery that failed is `ackOutbox`, which is the record's, and
               conflating them produces either a retry storm or a job never retried at all. */
            "max_retries": 3
          }
        ]
      },

      /* Outbound email. The record never holds the credential: ADR-062 puts every credential in
         the host. The sending domain is verified in the dashboard, once, per 51 A3. */
      "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],

      /* THE NIGHTLY COPIES GO HERE, one prefix per record (`<record>/snapshots/`), which is also
         what 51 §5.13 step 3 pages over when a customer is deleted. The bucket must exist before
         this deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs-alpha`. */
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "BLOBS", "bucket_name": "braindog-blobs-alpha" }],

      /* UNCOMMENTED IN THE COMMIT THAT FIRST READS IT, which is this one: `src/inference.ts` is the
         `Inference` port of 33 §1 over `env.AI`, and `src/inference.ts` alone reads this binding.

         THERE IS NO ACCOUNT RESOURCE TO CREATE FIRST, and that is what makes `ai` different from
         every other block below. A queue, a bucket and a Secrets Store store all have to exist
         before a deploy that names them, or the deploy fails. Workers AI is account-level: the
         binding resolves as long as Workers AI is enabled on the account. The three preconditions
         were established off this tree before the commit and are recorded here so the next reader
         does not go looking: Workers AI enabled, credits on the account, `ai (write)` on the deploy
         token. Nothing in this repository can check them, and no test here spends a neuron.

         IT IS NOT INHERITABLE, so it is written out again in `production` below. An omitted
         non-inheritable key is EMPTY rather than inherited — the same trap `r2_buckets` carries.

         `AI_GATEWAY` above is the other half. It names the gateway the calls are routed through, so
         neurons are counted and rate-limited per environment (12 §8), and `collectLog: false` in
         `inference.ts` is what keeps the payloads out of the log (11 §3). */
      "ai": { "binding": "AI" },

      /* THE SANDBOX A PERSON'S SCRIPT RUNS IN (`src/script.ts`). Dynamic
         Workers exist on Workers Paid only, and there is no account resource to create first: like `ai`, the binding
         resolves on any paid account.

         NOT INHERITABLE, and wrangler's own schema says so: "This field is not automatically inherited from the top
         level environment, and so must be specified in every named environment." So it is written out again in
         `production` below.

         NO `enable_ctx_exports` IN `compatibility_flags`, although the design first asked for one. `script.ts` needs
         `ctx.exports`, and at this file's `compatibility_date` it is already on: workerd 1.20260903.1, handed the flag,
         answered "The compatibility flag enable_ctx_exports became the default as of 2025-11-17 so does not need to be
         specified anymore". The array stays empty, and a flag that is already the default is not a diff worth having. */
      "worker_loaders": [{ "binding": "LOADER" }]

      /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         THE REST OF 51 §4.6, DRAFTED AND NOT ACTIVE. Each block below binds a resource the code
         does not yet read. Binding a resource that does not exist fails the deploy, so these are
         uncommented in the commit that first reads them, together with the account resource named
         beside each. Names carry the environment for the same reason the queue does — every one of
         these namespaces is account-global.

         `analytics_engine_datasets` IS THE ONE THE CODE ALREADY READS, and it stays drafted on purpose until the owner
         decides: one point per request is the owner's money once Analytics Engine bills for it, so
         binding it is the owner's choice and not a deploy's. `src/telemetry.ts` writes nothing while it is unbound, and with it
         bound writes one point per request once `SESSION_SECRET` is set too, because the record's hash is keyed off
         that secret. There is no account resource to create first: a dataset exists from its first write.
         "analytics_engine_datasets": [
           { "binding": "TELEMETRY", "dataset": "braindog_telemetry_alpha" }],

         `ratelimits` IS THE EXCEPTION: the code reads it now (`overLimit` in `src/index.ts`) and production binds it.
         Alpha leaves it drafted on purpose, kept for a rehearsal, and an unbound one limits nothing:
         "ratelimits": [
           { "name": "RL_READ",  "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 600, "period": 60 } },
           { "name": "RL_WRITE", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 120, "period": 60 } },
           { "name": "RL_POLL",  "namespace_id": "1003", "simple": { "limit": 240, "period": 60 } }],
         "secrets_store_secrets": [
           { "binding": "KEK", "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek-alpha" }]

         THERE IS NO `kv_namespaces` KEY IN EITHER ENVIRONMENT, AND THERE WILL NOT BE ONE. The
         record is the Durable Object's own SQLite (33 §7); the Directory is a Durable Object; the
         shell is `assets`; sessions, tokens and lockouts are rows in the Directory (33 §10). There
         is nothing left for a KV namespace to hold, and an eventually-consistent store beside a
         single-writer object is a second answer to questions that already have one.
         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    },

    /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       PRODUCTION — script `braindog-production`. Deployed from a tag, or by hand from
       `workflow_dispatch`, behind the `production` GitHub Environment's approval.

       NOTE THE SCRIPT NAME. A named environment deploys `<name>-<environment>`, so declaring these
       two environments RENAMES the production Worker from `braindog` to `braindog-production`. If
       `braindog` has ever been deployed, its Durable Objects stay with `braindog` and the new
       script starts empty: this is a migration, not a rename, and it is the one thing to settle
       before the first run of `deploy.yml`.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    "production": {
      /* `app.brain.dog` IS THIS WORKER'S CUSTOM DOMAIN AND THE APP'S ADDRESS, AT ITS ROOT: a record is
         `https://app.brain.dog/<slug>`. `brain.dog` stays the site's Custom Domain, and the site hands the
         app's doors and the old `/app` to this Worker through its `APP` service binding
         (`apps/site/wrangler.jsonc`), where a page load under `/app` is one 308 to
         `https://app.brain.dog<path>` with the `/app` taken off, and `/api/*`, `/mcp` and `/in/*` are
         served and never redirected, because the CLI refuses
         redirects, a webhook sender does not follow one, and a 301 or 302 turns a POST into a GET.
         `brain.dog` stays a legacy host for at least the 90 days a token lives and until every `/in/`
         address handed out on it is re-minted.

         `admin.brain.dog` IS THE OWNER'S ADMIN PAGE (`src/admin.ts`), its own Custom Domain, made by
         the next deploy once the owner chose it; Cloudflare Access can be put in front of it later with no code change. */
      "routes": [
        { "pattern": "app.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true },
        { "pattern": "admin.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }
      ],

      "vars": {
        /* THE CSRF ORIGIN, THE MAGIC LINK'S ORIGIN AND THE DEVICE FLOW'S HOST, ALL AT ONCE, and
           `app.brain.dog` from push C on. This is the change that makes everybody sign in once more: every
           cookie-authenticated write must now carry `Origin: https://app.brain.dog`, so a session cookie
           minted on `brain.dog` is useless here by construction. That is the `__Host-` cookie doing
           its job. `POST /auth/device` on `brain.dog` answers 403 "This BrainDog signs devices in
           at https://app.brain.dog, so start there",
           so the CLI's login example names this host, and `apps/cli/test/login.test.ts` reads it from
           here. */
        "PUBLIC_HOST": "app.brain.dog",
        /* THE NAMES THIS WORKER STILL ANSWERS AND NO LONGER LIVES ON, separated by commas. Each one must
           also be a Custom Domain in `routes` above, or nothing reaches the Worker to redirect. */
        "LEGACY_HOSTS": "brain.dog",
        /* THE PASSKEY'S RELYING PARTY IS THE PARENT FROM PUSH A ON, AND THE REASON IT IS NOT SIMPLY
           `PUBLIC_HOST`. A passkey is offered only where its RP ID is the host or a registrable suffix
           of it, so one made under RP ID `app.brain.dog` would never be offered on `brain.dog` after B2;
           made under `brain.dog` it works on both. Sign-in links are on in production (`/auth/methods`
           answers `magic:true`), so a stranded
           passkey is recoverable by mail, but it is still the owner's first passkey thrown away. This
           value must not change between pushes: a rollback to a version without it strands every
           passkey made since. `relyingParty` in `packages/host/src/auth.ts`
           switches passkeys off for an RP ID that is neither the host nor a dot-suffix of it, so
           `rain.dog` here would be a refusal an operator can read rather than a ceremony that fails in
           the browser (section 3.0 of the design). */
        "RP_ID": "brain.dog",
        /* THE OWNER'S APPLE DEVELOPER TEAM (ten capitals and digits, public in every app he signs), which
           `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` names both BrainDog apps under, the iPhone's (`dog.brain.app`) and
           the Mac's (`dog.brain.desktop`), so they may use the passkeys of `brain.dog` and `app.brain.dog` and the iPhone
           app opens the links it draws (`src/apple.ts`). Not a secret. Empty answers that file 404, and the apps'
           passkeys and links are refused while every other way in works. */
        "APPLE_TEAM_ID": "MG86668Z52",
        "AI_GATEWAY": "braindog",
        "AGE_RECIPIENT": "age1av0654ll7t2ghrzr7ekervs5hjc9sm0he0569z45cvu92dd43aqql9gm3t",
        "SLASH_ENDPOINT": "",
        /* THE INCLUDED MODEL IS ON, AT THE OWNER'S WORD. `apps/worker/src/slash.ts` answers
           with Workers AI only when this is `yes`; the binding alone never switched it on. One call may spend about 2,500
           neurons (about $0.03) before it is stopped, and one record's asks at most the ceiling below in a month. */
        "SLASH_INCLUDED": "yes",
        /* THE OPERATOR'S CEILING ON ONE RECORD'S ASKS OF Slash, as alpha's comment says: 300,000 neurons, about $3.30 a
           record a month, which `ask_cap` can only lower. It holds a person's asks and the record's own background asks
           apart, each to this number of asks, and the two together to this number of neurons, so one record never costs
           more than it. */
        "SLASH_INCLUDED_MONTH_NEURONS": "300000",
        /* THE ROUTE: SLASH_LARGE is the operator's yes to the larger model's cost, and empty is no. The three after it name
           the operator's second provider, asked when the included model fails, with its secret SLASH_FALLBACK_KEY; any
           one empty is no second provider, and they stay empty until the published legal text names it. */
        "SLASH_LARGE": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_URL": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_MODEL": "",
        "SLASH_FALLBACK_PROVIDER": "",
        "BRAINDOG_BUILD": "",
        /* EMPTY IS NO SIGN-IN LINKS. The sender, on a domain Email Service sends for, switches them on
           with the `EMAIL` binding below (51 §5.8). */
        "AUTH_MAIL_FROM": "hello@brain.dog",
        /* EMPTY FALLS BACK TO AUTH_MAIL_FROM: mail.ts reads it for everything but a sign-in link. */
        "MAIL_FROM": "",
        /* EMPTY FALLS BACK TO THE GENERIC FOOTER: mail.ts reads it for the address a reader is told to write to. */
        "SUPPORT_ADDRESS": "support@brain.dog",
        /* WHERE A `crash` OR `refuse` LINE IS MAILED, at most once a kind in 15 minutes (`opsAlert` in `src/mail.ts`).
           Production only: empty or absent is no mail, which is what alpha wants. */
        "OPS_ALERT_TO": "admin@brain.dog",
        /* AND THE ONE WORD THAT LETS A KEYLESS RECORD BE MADE ON PURPOSE. Empty is the default and
           the default is a refusal: with no `KEK` bound, `/auth/provision` answers
           `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` rather than making a record that can never seal a grant, because a
           record is given its keys once and the moment is the create. `yes` and nothing else turns
           that off, for a host that seals nothing. Set here or with
           `--var PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS:yes` on the deploy. */
        "PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS": "",
        /* TEAMS_HELD: `1` holds new invitations and joins on every plan (`TEAMS_HELD_SAID`), the switch to pull if sharing
           has to stop at once. Off since 1 Nov: member row rules, private rows, shares and the Admin's limits shipped, and
           a second person joins as the plan allows (`teamsStop` in `src/index.ts`), on any plan while billing is off. */
        "TEAMS_HELD": "",
        /* WEB PUSH'S PUBLIC KEY, AND EMPTY IS NO PUSH. It is a var and not a secret because the app hands it to every
           browser that subscribes. The private half is the secret `VAPID_PRIVATE` (the note at the end of this file),
           and `notify.push` is leased only when both are set (`delivers` in `src/deliver.ts`), so one without the
           other leaves pushes `pending`, as they have always been. Generated at the owner's word,
           one pair per environment; the private half lives only in the Worker secret and in his secrets
           folder, never in this repository. */
        "VAPID_PUBLIC": "BDFva-xw8GzY6aZWJ56lUpaec3IFwe545q2VcuYgEW6QgWkXeGiBUn2WPouNTuJx-_6lPyAcd9KezHlV8DbOE1k",
        /* BILLING IS OFF, AND THIS IS ITS SWITCH, as alpha's comment says. `live` here takes real cards, and is the owner's choice
           alone; the site's `LAUNCH` flags mirror this value, and a site test holds them equal. */
        "BILLING": "",
        /* THE STRIPE PRICES OF CLOUD AND CLOUD PLUS, as alpha's comment says, from the live mode of the owner's Stripe account. */
        "STRIPE_PRICE_HOSTED": "price_1UKmaqPop5yzbTmmSOx5842B",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PLUS": "price_1UKiNQPop5yzbTmm6IjGxQFd",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PACK_100": "price_1UTR8SPop5yzbTmmv8W5SDcQ",
        "STRIPE_PRICE_PACK_500": "price_1UTR8VPop5yzbTmmnUXsyt9O",
        /* EXTRA USAGE'S METERED PRICE AND ITS METER, and STRIPE TAX (`on` adds tax on Stripe's page). Empty is not sold / off.
           `node tools/ship/stripe.mjs` fills the price and the meter; STRIPE_TAX is the owner's switch once Stripe Tax is set up. */
        "STRIPE_PRICE_EXTRA": "price_1UWyboPop5yzbTmmvioaE1BT",
        "STRIPE_METER_EXTRA": "braindog_extra_cents",
        "STRIPE_TAX": "",
        /* WHO MAY MAKE AN ACCOUNT, as alpha's comment says. OPEN TO STRANGERS FROM 8 OCTOBER:
           with no bot check keyed, the strikes are tightened until it is. The site's `LAUNCH.signup` mirrors this value,
           and a site test holds them equal and fails when this line is missing.
           INVITE ONLY FROM 17 OCTOBER: a new address needs a code, minted at
           admin.brain.dog or by an application the owner invites, and the site still says "Start free". */
        "SIGNUP": "invite",
        /* THE SIGN-UP PAGE'S TURNSTILE WIDGET, as alpha's comment says. */
        "TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY": "0x4AAAAAAFvL6vpxsuMhToz5",
        /* FIRST_READ_DAILY: the day's ceiling of sign-up readings by the included model for all strangers, and the operator's yes to them. Unset is off, and the page reads by rules. 300, his yes to C7 (1 Nov, "i need it highly intelligent"). Still off in effect until `TURNSTILE_SECRET` is set: the door reads nothing behind no bot check. */
        "FIRST_READ_DAILY": "300",
        /* SHARE_LINKS: `on` makes read-only links to a page (`sharesOf` in `src/share.ts`), with `KEK` bound. Unset, and none are made, and every open link stops answering. On since 1 Nov (his ask of 20 Oct, sharing finished). */
        "SHARE_LINKS": "on",
        /* COUNT_VISITS: `yes` counts the site's visits by day, page and where they came from, and nothing about who (`src/seen.ts`). Unset, and `/api/seen` answers 204 and counts nothing. On in production since 23 October. */
        "COUNT_VISITS": "yes",
        /* THE OPERATOR'S CANARY RECORD, as alpha's comment says, and empty until the same sitting: the line
           `tools/canary/enrol.mjs` prints goes here, in the commit after his sitting, and from then the sandbox smoke
           and `after-deploy.yml` both run on it. */
        "OPS_RECORD": ""
      },

      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "RECORD", "class_name": "Record$" },
          { "name": "DIRECTORY", "class_name": "Directory$" }
        ]
      },

      "queues": {
        "producers": [{ "queue": "braindog-ingress", "binding": "INGRESS" }],
        "consumers": [
          {
            "queue": "braindog-ingress",
            "max_batch_size": 25,
            "max_batch_timeout": 5,
            "max_retries": 3
          }
        ]
      },

      "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],

      /* REPEATED IN FULL, because `r2_buckets` is not inheritable and an omitted non-inheritable
         key is EMPTY rather than inherited: production would deploy, come up with no bucket, and
         the first sign of it would be `scheduled()` throwing at 03:17. The bucket must exist before
         this deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs`. */
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "BLOBS", "bucket_name": "braindog-blobs" }],

      /* REPEATED IN FULL, for the reason `r2_buckets` above is: `ai` is not inheritable, and an
         omitted non-inheritable key is empty rather than inherited. Production without this line
         deploys, comes up, and answers `ModelUnavailable` to the first ask Slash makes. The
         argument for uncommenting it is in `alpha` above; there is no account resource to create
         for it, unlike every block still commented below. */
      "ai": { "binding": "AI" },

      /* REPEATED IN FULL, for the same reason: `worker_loaders` is not inheritable. Production without it deploys and
         meters every script run `dropped`, saying this host has no sandbox. The argument is in `alpha` above. */
      "worker_loaders": [{ "binding": "LOADER" }],

      /* THE FRONT DOORS' TWO BUDGETS, READ BY CODE AND SO BOUND HERE (`overLimit` in `src/index.ts`): one caller may ask
         600 times a minute and write 120 times a minute, keyed by the token, the person, or for `/auth/*` the address.
         The numbers are `READ_PER_MINUTE` and `WRITE_PER_MINUTE` in `packages/host/src/limits.ts`, which the self-host
         counts to, and a change to one here that is not made there gives the two hosts different ceilings.

         PRODUCTION ONLY. `alpha` keeps its block drafted and commented, and an unbound binding limits nothing, so alpha
         answers as it did. NOT INHERITABLE, like every key in this block: an environment that should have it writes it.
         A rate-limit namespace is not a resource to create first; `namespace_id` is a number this account chooses, and
         a 429 `RATE_LIMITED` with `Retry-After: 60` is what a caller over it is answered. */
      "ratelimits": [
        { "name": "RL_READ", "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 600, "period": 60 } },
        { "name": "RL_WRITE", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 120, "period": 60 } },
        /* THE VERSION POLL'S OWN MINUTE (`POLL_PER_MINUTE`): an open tab asks `/api/version` every 1.5 s while it has
           focus, and that must never be refused because the same person's screens spent the read budget. Unbound, the
           poll spends `RL_READ` as it did before (`bindingLimiter`). */
        { "name": "RL_POLL", "namespace_id": "1003", "simple": { "limit": 240, "period": 60 } },
        /* THE MCP DOOR'S OWN MINUTE FOR ONE TOKEN (`MCP_PER_MINUTE`, 300), so an agent loop is stopped without spending the
           reads a person's screens share. Unbound, /mcp spends `RL_READ` as it did before. The namespace is made by the deploy. */
        { "name": "RL_MCP", "namespace_id": "1004", "simple": { "limit": 300, "period": 60 } }
      ]

      /* The same drafted blocks as alpha, without the suffix, less `ratelimits`, which is bound above. `TELEMETRY` waits
         for the owner's decision here as in alpha, and the argument is there:
         "analytics_engine_datasets": [
           { "binding": "TELEMETRY", "dataset": "braindog_telemetry" }],
         "secrets_store_secrets": [
           { "binding": "KEK", "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek" }]  */
    }
  }

  /* WORKER SECRETS ARE NOT IN THIS FILE AND NEVER WILL BE. `SESSION_SECRET` and
     `TOKENS_VALID_FROM` (12 §8's two fleet-wide levers, 53 §5.11's incident response) are set with
     `wrangler secret put <NAME> --env <environment>`, value at the prompt and never as an
     argument (53 §2.2). They are PER SCRIPT, so alpha and production hold different values —
     which is the point: a shared `SESSION_SECRET` would make a session minted in alpha valid in
     production, since the session hash is an HMAC under it.

     SIGN-IN SHIPS SWITCHED OFF, AND THESE ARE ITS SWITCHES. Without `SESSION_SECRET` (32 characters or
     more) `/auth/methods` says `not-configured` and every other `/auth` route is 404. `PROVISION_KEY`
     is the third secret: the operator's bearer for `/auth/provision` and `/auth/claim/mint`, which make a
     record and hand back its 24-hour claim link; without it both are 404.

     `KEK` IS THE FOURTH, AND THE KEY DOORS' SWITCH (12 §6, 51 §8 rule 3). Its name is declared here and its value
     never is: 32 bytes as 64 hex characters or base64url, a different value per environment, put at the prompt with
     `wrangler secret put KEK --env <environment>`, or bound from Secrets Store by the drafted
     `secrets_store_secrets` block above under the same name, never both. Without it `/api/vault/*` answers
     `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` after checking the grant, and `/auth/provision` REFUSES rather than making a keyless record.
     With it, records provisioned from then on get their three keys, and one made before keeps none. Amend 12 §6,
     51 §8 rule 3 and 16 ADR-062 in the same revision: all three said provisioning carried on without keys, which
     was true until the guard in `src/keyless.ts` landed. The reason for the change is that the mistake cannot be
     undone — a record created with no `vault_key` rows never gets them, because a second create would overwrite the
     DEK every sealed grant was made under — so the only repair is to throw the Directory name away, and 52 §13 has
     no undo for one. `PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS` in `vars` is the deliberate exception. It is deliberately not
     listed under `secrets.required`: that key fails the first deploy of an environment while the secret is unset,
     and unset is a supported state for every door but that one.

     `SUPPORT_FORWARD` IS THE LAST, AND A SECRET ONLY BECAUSE IT IS HIS OWN INBOX: where mail to the support address is
     forwarded, put with `wrangler secret put SUPPORT_FORWARD --env <environment>`. Unset, that mail is refused at the door.

     A ROTATION IS THE SAME SECRET HOLDING TWO KEYS, the new one, a space, and the old one (`src/keys.ts`, 53 §5.6),
     and never a second binding: `KEK_PREV`, which 53 §5.6 still names, is read by nothing.
     New keys are wrapped under the first at once and every record still opens under either. `rewrapRecord` in
     `src/rewrap.ts` moves one record's keys under the first and answers `straggler` for a record it could not move,
     and it is the nightly walk's to call for every record. Put the new key back alone only after a night whose walk
     re-wrapped every record with no stragglers, and not while a copy made before the rotation may still be restored,
     because a key the secret no longer holds opens nothing wrapped under it.

     `VAPID_PRIVATE` IS THE FIFTH, AND WEB PUSH'S SWITCH TOGETHER WITH THE `VAPID_PUBLIC` VAR. It is the 32-byte P-256
     private key in base64url, the second of the two strings a VAPID key generator prints, put with
     `wrangler secret put VAPID_PRIVATE --env <environment>` and its public half set as `VAPID_PUBLIC` in the same
     environment's `vars`. A different pair per environment, so a subscription made on alpha is refused by production.
     Unset, pushes stay `pending` and nothing else changes; set with a public key from another run, every push
     dead-letters with a sentence that names both settings (`vapidKeys` in `src/vapid.ts`). Not under
     `secrets.required`, for the reason `KEK` is not.

     `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` AND `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` ARE BILLING'S TWO, and `TURNSTILE_SECRET` IS SIGN-UP'S.
     The first is Stripe's secret key, `sk_test_...` while `BILLING` is `test` and
     `sk_live_...` only once the owner turns billing live; the second is the signing secret Stripe shows for the endpoint
     `https://<PUBLIC_HOST>/account/billing/webhook`, which the webhook checks every event against (`verifyStripe` in
     `packages/host/src/stripe-sign.ts`); the third is the secret half of `TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY`. Each is put with
     `wrangler secret put <NAME> --env <environment>`, a different value per environment. Unset, billing answers
     `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` naming the missing one and sign-up runs on its lockouts alone; a key whose mode is not
     `BILLING`'s refuses every billing door, naming both. None is under `secrets.required`, for the reason `KEK` is not.

     `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` AND `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` ARE SIGN-IN WITH GOOGLE'S TWO (`src/google.ts`): the id and the
     secret of one OAuth web client whose redirect is `https://<PUBLIC_HOST>/auth/google/callback`, each put with
     `wrangler secret put <NAME> --env <environment>`. Either unset, and the sign-in page offers no Google and both
     routes are 404. Not under `secrets.required`, for the reason `KEK` is not.

     CONNECT GOOGLE USES THE SAME CLIENT (`src/google-connect.ts`): add `https://<PUBLIC_HOST>/api/google/callback` as a
     second authorised redirect URI on it, and the Gmail, Calendar and Contacts read-only scopes to its consent screen.
     Either secret unset, and Connect Google says it is not set up here. Its refresh tokens are sealed under each
     record's connector key, so `KEK` must be bound too. */
}
